WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.871
Least privilege means providing users, devices, applications,

00:00:02.871 --> 00:00:06.830
and services only the access needed to perform authorized functions.

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:11.581
It reduces the amount of unnecessary capability available if an account, device,

00:00:11.581 --> 00:00:13.165
or process is misused.

00:00:13.165 --> 00:00:16.728
Zero Trust applies least privilege at the resource level.

00:00:16.728 --> 00:00:21.479
Instead of granting broad access because a request crossed a network boundary,

00:00:21.479 --> 00:00:25.834
policy evaluates whether the requester needs this particular application, service, function,

00:00:25.834 --> 00:00:27.022
or data set.

00:00:27.022 --> 00:00:32.168
Permissions should reflect role and context, but roles alone can become too broad.

00:00:32.168 --> 00:00:36.127
Organizations may combine job responsibilities with resource attributes, device posture,

00:00:36.127 --> 00:00:38.899
risk signals, time, purpose, and approval requirements.

00:00:38.899 --> 00:00:44.441
Privileged access deserves stronger governance because administrative actions can affect many systems or users.

00:00:44.441 --> 00:00:48.005
Clear ownership, separate administrative roles, appropriate approval, limited duration,

00:00:48.005 --> 00:00:50.380
and detailed monitoring can reduce exposure.

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:53.547
Service and workload accounts also require least privilege.

00:00:53.547 --> 00:00:57.902
Long-lived credentials and broad machine permissions can become invisible operational dependencies,

00:00:57.902 --> 00:01:01.861
so teams need inventories, ownership, review, and controlled lifecycle management.

00:01:01.861 --> 00:01:04.633
Access is not a one-time design decision.

00:01:04.633 --> 00:01:08.592
Responsibilities change, projects end, applications retire, and temporary needs expire.

00:01:08.592 --> 00:01:13.343
Periodic review helps remove access that is no longer justified and confirms

00:01:13.343 --> 00:01:16.510
that important entitlements still have an accountable owner.

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:21.261
Usability matters because controls that obstruct normal work can encourage unsafe workarounds.

00:01:21.261 --> 00:01:26.011
A well-designed program makes the appropriate path understandable and efficient while reserving

00:01:26.011 --> 00:01:27.991
additional friction for higher-risk decisions.

00:01:27.991 --> 00:01:31.950
Least privilege does not mean minimum access at any cost.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:35.909
It means access that is sufficient for the approved task,

00:01:35.909 --> 00:01:40.660
limited in scope and duration, and supported by policy, monitoring, and review.
